<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Speyer</h1><p class="st-meta">1111 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Bruchsal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A prince-bishopric on the Rhine whose cathedral holds the tombs of Salian emperors, its bishops ruling from Bruchsal until 1803.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Speyer</a> had been a bishopric since the 7th century, and Salian kings made it their family church; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Conrad II</a> began the great cathedral around 1030. In 1111 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Henry V</a> granted the citizens of Speyer privileges, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' secular rule over their territory developed in the 11th and 12th centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ruled a territory on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> but not the city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Speyer</a>, which became a free imperial city; they moved their residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippsburg">Udenheim</a>, renamed Philippsburg, and in 1720 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruchsal">Bruchsal</a>, where Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damian-hugo-philipp-von-schonborn">Damian Hugo von Schönborn</a> built a baroque palace. The cathedral, burial place of eight emperors and kings, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the left-bank lands during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">Revolutionary Wars</a>, and in 1803 the right-bank territory, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruchsal">Bruchsal</a>, was secularised and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. The diocese of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Speyer</a> was re-founded in 1817 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-speyer.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Speyer" width="320" height="377"><figcaption>Image: Nicholas de Fer, 1705 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Worms_and_Speyer_Bishoprics_1705.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1111 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bruchsal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Damian Hugo von Schönborn, August of Limburg-Stirum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Rhine Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Speyer</dd></dl></aside></div>
